## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to orchestrate low-downtime upgrades in SAP on AWS with native services, focusing on reliability, rollback, and measurable validation.

## Key Concepts
- Blue/Green deployments for SAP on EC2
- Route 53 weighted routing and health checks
- SSM automation for kernel patching
- CloudFormation for repeatable environments
- Validation: SAP status, HANA health, ABAP batch

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: update Route 53 weighted record to switch traffic
const { Route53Client, ChangeResourceRecordSetsCommand } = require('@aws-sdk/client-route-53');
const client = new Route53Client({ region: 'us-east-1' });
async function switchTraffic(hostedZoneId, recordName, weight) {
  const params = {
    HostedZoneId: hostedZoneId,
    ChangeBatch: {
      Changes: [
        {
          Action: 'UPSERT',
          ResourceRecordSet: {
            Name: recordName,
            Type: 'A',
            SetIdentifier: weight > 50 ? 'Green' : 'Blue',
            Weight: weight,
            TTL: 60
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  };
  return client.send(new ChangeResourceRecordSetsCommand(params));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate rollback safety if the Green stack patch causes SAP instability?
- What metrics would you capture to decide promotion vs rollback?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a beginner's ability to design a practical, auditable maintenance workflow using familiar AWS services with least-privilege access.

## Key Concepts

- AWS Systems Manager Run Command
- IAM roles with trust relationships and least privilege
- CloudWatch Logs and CloudTrail for audit trails
- AWS SSO integration for role assumption
- Minimal IaC (Terraform) to create roles and SSM associations

## Code Example

```terraform
# Terraform snippet creating cross-account SAPOperator role and IAM policy
provider "aws" { region = "us-east-1" }

# Role
resource "aws_iam_role" "sap_operator" {
  name = "SAPOperator"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17",
    Statement = [{
      Effect = "Allow",
      Principal = { AWS = "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_ID:saml-provider/AWS_SSO" },
      Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
    }]
  })
}

# Policy
resource "aws_iam_policy" "sap_operator_policy" {
  name = "SAPOperatorPolicy"
  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17",
    Statement = [
      { Effect = "Allow", Action = ["ssm:SendCommand","ssm:ListCommands","ssm:DescribeInstanceInformation"], Resource = "*" },
      { Effect = "Allow", Action = ["logs:CreateLogGroup","logs:CreateLogStream","logs:PutLogEvents"], Resource = "*" }
    ]
  })
}

resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "attach" {
  role       = aws_iam_role.sap_operator.name
  policy_arn = aws_iam_policy.sap_operator_policy.arn
}
```

```terraform
# SSM association to run healthcheck on SAP EC2
data "aws_ssm_document" "run_shell" { name = "AWS-RunShellScript" }

resource "aws_ssm_association" "healthcheck" {
  name            = data.aws_ssm_document.run_shell.name
  instance_id     = "<SAP_EC2_INSTANCE_ID>"
  parameters = {
    commands = ["bash /usr/local/bin/healthcheck.sh"]
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical understanding of cross‑account IAM with MFA, least privilege, and IaC integration in a real SAP on AWS scenario.

## Key Concepts

- STS assumeRole with MFA
- IAM trust policies and cross‑account access
- Least privilege for SAP HANA actions
- Terraform for IAM role and trust policy
- CloudTrail auditing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Terraform: IAM role with MFA‑enforced trust
resource "aws_iam_role" "sap_operator" {
  name = "sap-operator"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = { AWS = "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B_ID:root" }
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
        Condition = { aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent = "true" }
      }
    ]
  })
}
```

```javascript
// Terraform: least-privilege SAP actions
resource "aws_iam_role_policy" "sap_operator_policy" {
  name = "sap-operator-policy"
  role = aws_iam_role.sap_operator.id
  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow"
        Action = ["sap:Read", "sap:Execute"]
        Resource = "*"
      }
    ]
  })
}
```

```javascript
# CLI test: assume role with MFA and perform a harmless SAP action
aws sts assume-role --role-arn arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A_ID:role/sap_operator --role-session-name sap-test \
  --serial-number arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B_ID:mfa/user --token-code 123456
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate ongoing policy least-privilege using AWS Config or IAM Access Analyzer?
- What changes would you make to rotate credentials or rotate the operator role across regions?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to map SAP transport governance to an AWS-native, beginner-friendly pipeline with clear gates and minimal risk.

## Key Concepts

- AWS CodeCommit, CodePipeline, S3
- Lambda validation logic and idempotency
- Manual approvals and CloudWatch alarms
- SAP transport basics (name conventions, MD5/signature)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lambda that validates an ABAP transport before import
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const {transportKey, metadata} = event;
  const validPattern = /^(Z|PRD)\./;
  if (!validPattern.test(transportKey)) throw new Error("Invalid transport name");
  if (!metadata.signature) throw new Error("Missing signature");
  // placeholder for real signature check
  return {status: "ok"};
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

